@page "/static-css"

<PageTitle>Static sites</PageTitle>

<RetroWindow Title="Static sites" ShowCloseButton="false">
    <h1>Static sites (HTML, PHP...)</h1>

    <p>
        The RetroOS looks can also be used <b>without Blazor</b>, in any static site (plain HTML, PHP, WordPress, Hugo...).
        There is <b>one stylesheet per OS</b>, all styling the <b>same</b> <code>retro-</code> classes, plus <b>retroos.css</b>
        with every look in one file. No JavaScript is needed.
    </p>

    <p>
        These files are <b>generated automatically</b> on each build of the library from the components' sources,
        so they always match the Blazor components. They are in the
        <a href="https://github.com/tossnet/Blazor-RetroOS/tree/main/static/dist" target="_blank">static/dist</a> folder of the repository.
    </p>

    <h2>Installation guide</h2>

    <h4>1. Get the files</h4>

    <p><b>Option A - CDN</b>, nothing to download:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@CdnCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Html" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <p><code>@@main</code> always serves the latest version. To freeze a version, replace <code>main</code> with a commit hash or a tag.</p>

    <p>
        <b>Option B - copy the files</b>: download the repository
        (<a href="https://github.com/tossnet/Blazor-RetroOS/archive/refs/heads/main.zip" target="_blank">ZIP</a>)
        and copy the <b>static/dist</b> folder into your site. Keep <b>fonts/</b> next to the stylesheets:
        they load the pixel fonts with relative paths.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@TreeCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Bash" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <h4>2. Load ONE theme</h4>

    <p>In the <b>&lt;head&gt;</b> of your pages:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@LinkCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Html" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <p>Every theme styles the same classes: to change the look, change the <code>href</code>, nothing else.</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@SwitchCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Html" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <h4>Several looks on the same page</h4>

    <p>
        Load <b>retroos.css</b> instead and choose the look with the class <code>retro-appearance-&lt;os&gt;</code> on any element:
        it applies to the element and to everything inside it. Without class, the look is Workbench 1.3.
        It is the equivalent of the <code>Appearance</code> parameter.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@BundleCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Html" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <h4>3. Write the components</h4>

    <p>Each component is plain HTML with <code>retro-</code> classes, the same markup the Blazor components render:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ComponentsCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Html" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <p>
        The complete table of classes and the CSS variables (<code>--retro-font</code>, <code>--retro-accent</code>...) are in the
        <a href="https://github.com/tossnet/Blazor-RetroOS/tree/main/static#readme" target="_blank">static sites guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h4>With PHP</h4>

    <p>Nothing special: the stylesheets are static files, PHP only generates the HTML. A small helper avoids repeating the classes:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@PhpCode" Language="CodeLanguage.Bash" EnableLineNumbers="false" />

    <h2>Demo</h2>

    <p>
        The demo below is a plain HTML page that only loads the RetroOS stylesheets. Change the theme with the buttons
        at the top, and click <b>HTML</b> under each component to get its markup.
        <a href="static/index.html" target="_blank">Open it in a new tab</a>.
    </p>

    <iframe src="static/index.html" title="RetroOS static CSS demo" class="static-demo"></iframe>

    <h2>Updating</h2>

    <p>
        Download the new <b>static/dist</b> folder again, or keep <code>@@main</code> in the CDN links.
        Do not edit the files of <b>static/dist</b>: to customize the styles, add your own stylesheet <b>after</b> the theme.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>

    <p>
        Only the look is provided: the gadgets of the windows (close, depth) and the buttons do nothing by themselves.
        Add your own JavaScript for the behavior (open, close, drag...) and read the values with the DOM.
    </p>
</RetroWindow>
